  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", is tested on two replies. Reply A is the text: '```html\\n{\"a\": 1}\\n```\\n{\"b\": 2}'. Reply B is the text: '{\"b\": 2}'. The rule requires Reply A to be scored as {\"a\": 1}, because the last object inside a code fence (the ```html fence) is {\"a\": 1}, and Reply B to be scored as {\"b\": 2}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\": 2} for Reply A as well, because its fence pattern `_FENCE = re.compile('```(?:json|JSON)?\\\\s*(.*?)```', re.DOTALL)` matches only unlabeled, ```json, or ```JSON fences, and does not recognize the ```html fence. Thus the fenced pool is empty for Reply A, and the check falls back to the last top-level object, which is {\"b\": 2}. This observation would show the conjecture false if the check actually returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A, meaning the ```html fence was recognized as a code fence."
